          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One of the most important observations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is that large parts of mathematics can be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalized
            in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with sufficient structure. The most basic examples of this involve algebraic structures; for instance, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            can be defined in any category with finite products, and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict category
            can be defined in any ambient category with
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic
            (or even
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic
            %em operations
            with
            %em equational axioms
            imposed, it suffices for the ambient category to have (usually finite) limits.
          %p
            However, if we assume that the ambient category has additional structure, then much more of mathematics can be internalized, potentially including
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
          %p
            The basic ideas of the internal logic induced by a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are regarded as collections of things of a given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are regarded as terms of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                containing a free
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable"} variable
                of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                (i.e. in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \hookrightarrow A
                is regarded as a
                %em proposition
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"}> predicate
                ): by thinking of it as the sub-collection of all those things of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                for which the statement
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the maximal subobject is hence the proposition that is always true; this is the logical object of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊤
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \hookrightarrow A
                %li
                  %p
                    the minimal subobject is hence the proposition that is always false; this is the logical object of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsity"} falsity
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bottom \hookrightarrow A
                %li
                  %p
                    one proposition
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implies
                    another if as subobjects of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    they are connected by a morphism in the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                    of subobjects:
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi ψ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \Rightarrow \psi
                    means
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi ψ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \hookrightarrow \psi
            %li
              %p
                Logical operations are implemented by
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the conjunction
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/and"} and
                    is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                    of subobjects (their
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/or"} or
                    is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
                    of subobjects (their
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
                    is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
                    of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
                    of subobjects
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
                    is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
                    of subobjects
          %p and so on.
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
              over an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              may be interpreted as a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to A
              whose “fibers” represent the types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(x)
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+map"} display map
              or a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
          %p
            Once we formalize the notion of “logical
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lang
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang
            from suitably structured categories to theories. The morphisms of theories are “interpretations”, and so an internalization of some theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (such as the “theory of groups”) into a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a morphism of theories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Lang
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to Lang(C)
          %p
            Moreover, the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lang
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang
            has a left
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Syn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Syn
            of a theory. Thus, a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equally well a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Syn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Syn(T)\to C
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h3#kinds_of_internal_logics_in_different_categories Kinds of internal logics in different categories
          %p
            There are many different kinds of of “logical theories”, each of which corresponds to a type of category in which such theories can be internalized (and yields a corresponding adjunction
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Syn
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Lang
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Syn \dashv Lang
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Theory
                %th
                %th Category
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere
                  (or “finite product”)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  category with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+logic"}> cartesian
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic
                  (or “left exact” or “finite limit”)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+logic"} minimal logic
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"} coherent
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunctive+logic"} disjunctive
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-lextensive+category"} pre-lextensive category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} infinitary coherent category
                  (aka
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+category"} geometric category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+logic"} higher order
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} elementary topos
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+logic"} higher order
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive modal logic
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
          %p
            Each type of logic up to and including “geometric” can also be described in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketches
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/familial+regularity+and+exactness"} familial regularity and exactness
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategory"} cartesian multicategory
          %p
            However, there are other sorts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            that do not fit in this framework. For instance, to describe a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            internal to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            for a discussion of the more general notion in the context of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
          %h2#internal_firstorder_logic Internal first-order logic
          %p
            We begin with the interpretation of internal first-order logic, which is the most used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            and related categories.
          %h3#theories Theories
          %p
            In this section, what we mean by a
            %em theory
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            without dependent types, but with a dependent logic. This entails the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
                of the theory consists of
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Various
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                            %mo ,
                            %mi C
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C
                    %em type constructors
                    that build new types from basic ones, such as product types
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
                    and the unit type
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                %li
                  %p
                    The theory will also generally contain
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+symbol"}> function symbol
                      s
                    such as
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi f
                            %mo :
                            %mi A
                            %mo →
                            %mi B
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi M
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi m
                            %mo :
                            %mi M
                            %mo ×
                            %mi M
                            %mo →
                            %mi M
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:M\times M\to M
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi e
                            %mo :
                            %mn 1
                            %mo →
                            %mi M
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:1\to M
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                    are also called
                    = succeed "." do
                      %em constants
                %li
                  %p
                    The theory may also contain
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+symbol"}> relation symbol
                      s
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi R
                            %mo :
                            %mi A
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R:A
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
                    has one type
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                    and one relation
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo :
                            %mi P
                            %mo ×
                            %mi P
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le:P\times P
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo =
                            %mi A
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =_A:A\times A
                    on a type
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                Finally the theory may contain
                %em
                  logical
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
                of the form
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi φ
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | \varphi  \vdash \psi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                are first-order formulas built up from terms and relation symbols using logical connectives and quantifiers such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ∀
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ∃
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top,\bot,\wedge,\vee,\Rightarrow,\neg,\forall,\exists
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                which declares the type of every variable occurring in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
          %p
            For example, the theory of a group has one type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:G\times G\to G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:G\to G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:1\to G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ⊤
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ⊤
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi e
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∧
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi e
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ⊤
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi e
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∧
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi e
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  x:G,y:G,z:G |  \top \vdash m(m(x,y),z) = m(x,m(y,z))\\
                  x:G | \top \vdash m(x,i(x)) = e \;\wedge\; m(i(x),x) = e\\
                  x:G | \top \vdash m(x,e) = x \;\wedge\; m(e,x) = x.
                  }
          %p
            This is an
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equational+theory"} equational theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le:P\times P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ⊤
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi x
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi y
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∧
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi y
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo :
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi y
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∧
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi y
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi z
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi z
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  x:P | \top \vdash x\le x\\
                  x:P,y:P | x\le y \;\wedge\; y\le x \vdash x=y\\
                  x:P,y:P,z:P | x\le y \;\wedge\; y\le z \vdash x\le z.
                  }
          %h3#CategoricalSemantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            Now suppose that we have a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite limit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p
            First, for each
            %em type
            in the theory we choose an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em function symbol
            in the theory we choose a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em relation
            in the theory we choose a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em equality
            on a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by the diagonal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\hookrightarrow A\times A
            in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            we must choose an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:G\times G\to G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:G\to G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:1\to G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and a subobject
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi P
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\le] \hookrightarrow P\times P
          %p
            Of course, this is not enough; we need to say somehow that
            = succeed "." do
              %em the axioms are satisfied
            %em term
            that can be constructed from the theory by a morphism in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:G\times G\to G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times G\times G \to G
            which are the interpretations of the two terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(m(x,y),z)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(x,m(y,z))
          %p
            We then define, inductively, an interpretation of every
            %em
              %span.newWikiWord
                logical formula
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/logical+formula"} ?
            that can be constructed from the theory by a
            %em subobject
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            is a variable of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x)
            is a formula with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as its free variable, then the interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x)
            should be the “subset”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\in A | \varphi(x)\}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is a term interpreted by a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R:B
            is a relation symbol, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(t)
            is interpreted by the pullback of the chosen subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\hookrightarrow B
            representing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            along the morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:A\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            of subobjects in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Logical operator
                %th
                %th
                  Operation on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"}> conjunction
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∧
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
                  (pullback)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"}> truth
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+element"} top element
                  (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  itself)
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"}> disjunction
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∨
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsity"}> falsity
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+element"} bottom element
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"}> strict initial object
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"}> implication
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⇒
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting implication
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"}> existential quantification
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∃
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                  to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"}> universal quantification
                  \:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∀
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                  to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            The fact that existential and universal quantifiers can be interpreted as left and right adjoints to pullbacks was first realized by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\subset A
            under a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            can be defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{b\in B | (\exists a\in A)(a\in R \;\wedge\; f(a)=b)\},
          %p while its “dual image” (the right adjoint to pullback) can be defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{b\in B | (\forall a \in A)(f(a)=b \Rightarrow a\in R)\}.
          %p
            Of course, not in all finitely complete categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            do all these operations on subobjects exist. Moreover, in order for the relationship with logic to be well-behaved, any of the operations we make use of must be
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+stability"} stable
              under
            (preserved by) pullbacks. (Pullbacks of subobjects correspond to “innocuous” logical operations such as adding extra unused variables, duplicating variables, and so on, so they should definitely not affect the meaning of the logical connectives. However, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            such operations become less innocuous.)
          %p
            In any category with finite limits, the posets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            always have finite intersections (given by pullback), including a top element (given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            itself). Thus in any such category, we can interpret logical theories that use only the connectives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            = succeed "In" do
              %br/
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*:Sub(B)\to Sub(A)
            along any map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            has a left adjoint, usually written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
          %p
            Actually, some instances of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            can be interpreted in any category with finite limits: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is itself a monomorphism, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            always has a left adjoint, given simply by composition with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            %em cartesian logic
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em finite-limit logic
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            is basically defined to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            in which the subobject posets additionally have pullback-stable finite unions. Thus, in a coherent category we can interpret so-called
            = succeed "," do
              %em coherent logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em geometric logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi φ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee_i \varphi_i
            to coherent logic. Geometric logic is especially important because it is preserved by the inverse image parts of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
          %p
            On the other hand, in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} lextensive category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            which are
            %em disjoint
            (their intersection is initial), then their coproduct is also their union in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            = succeed "," do
              %em disjunctive logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            and “provably disjoint disjunction.” Likewise, in an infinitary-lextensive category we can interpret “infinitary-disjunctive logic.”
          %p
            Finally, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            the base change functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*:Sub(B)\to Sub(A)
            also have right adjoints, usually written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            is also a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            as well. (We define “negation” by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi φ
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi φ
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \varphi \equiv \varphi \Rightarrow \bot
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
          %p
            Now that we know how to interpret logic, we can say that a
            %strong model
            of a given theory in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of a choice of objects, morphisms, and subobjects for the types, function symbols, and relation symbols as above, such that for each axiom
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | \varphi \vdash \psi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]\le [\psi]
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub([\Gamma])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Gamma]
            is the product of the objects that correspond to the types of the variables in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\psi]
            are the interpretations of the formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            as subobjects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Gamma]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            is the relation of subobject inclusion.
          %p
            It is easy to verify that a model of the theory of a group in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is precisely an internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            object in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:G,y:G,z:G | \top \vdash m(m(x,y),z) = m(x,m(y,z))
          %p
            means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            of the two morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times G\times G \to G
            must be all of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times G\times G
          %h3#the_internal_logic_as_a_functor The internal logic as a functor
          %p
            As described above, a model of a given theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of an assignment
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th
                %th
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  types of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  objects of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  function symbols of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  morphisms of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  relation symbols of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  subobjects in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  axioms of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  containments in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            This is a sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphism"} heteromorphism
            in that it changes the name of things as it operates on them. We can describe it more simply as a “translation of theories” as follows.
          %p
            Given a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (which may be regular, coherent, geometric, Heyting, etc.), we define its
            %strong internal type theory (with first-order logic)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lang
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang(C)
            to be the theory whose
          %ul
            %li
              types are the objects of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              function symbols are the morphisms on
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              relation symbols are the subobjects in
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              axioms are the containments in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Now a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be described simply as a morphism of theories (a “translation” or “interpretation”)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Lang
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Lang(C).
          %p
            The functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lang
                  %mo :
                  %mi Categories
                  %mo →
                  %mi Theories
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang : Categories \to Theories
            has a left adjoint, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            of a theory. Thus we have a chain of natural isomorphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Theories
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Lang
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Models
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Categories
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Syn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Theories(T,Lang(C)) \cong Models(T,C) \cong Categories(Syn(T),C)).
          %h2#soundness_and_internal_reasoning Soundness and internal reasoning
          %p
            Internal logic is not just a way to concisely describe internal structures in a category, but also gives us a way to
            %em prove
            things about them by “internal reasoning.” We simply need to verify that the “usual” methods of logical reasoning (for example, from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\vdash \psi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi\vdash \chi
            deduce
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\vdash\chi
            = succeed "," do
              %em internally valid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (in the example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]\le [\psi]
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\psi]\le [\chi]
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]\le [\chi]
            = succeed "." do
              %em Soundness Theorem
          %p
            It then follows that if we start from the axioms of a theory and “reason normally” within type theory, which in practice amounts to pretending that the types are sets, the function symbols are functions, and the relation symbols are subsets, then anything we prove will still be true when the theory is interpreted in an
            %em arbitrary
            category, not just
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:G,y:G,z:G | m(x,y)=e \;\wedge\; m(x,z)=e \vdash y=z.
          %p
            It follows that this is also true, suitably interpreted, as a statement about internal group objects in
            %em any
            category.
          %p
            There are (at least) three caveats. Firstly, we must take care to use only the rules appropriate to the fragment of logic that is valid in the particular categories we are interested in. For example, if we want our conclusions to be valid in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
          %p
            The second, more important, caveat is that the internal logic of all these categories is, in general,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg\varphi
            is, in general, distinct from that of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\vee \neg\varphi
            is not always valid. So even if we believe that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            (including the principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and even the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean
            and/or satisfies the internal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "es)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %p
            The third caveat is that one must take care to distinguish the
            %em internal
            logic of a category from what is
            %em externally
            true about it. In general, internal validity is “local” truth, meaning things which become true “after passing to a
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %em disjunction
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em existence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
            in the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a different statement from its being
            %em internally
            projective, meaning that “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is projective” is true in the internal logic. Another good example can be found in the different notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+object"} finite object
            in a topos. This problem goes away if the ambient category is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            itself.
          %h2#syntactic_categories Completeness, syntactic categories, and Morita equivalence
          %p
            The converse of the Soundness Theorem is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em Completeness Theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\vdash\psi
            is valid in every model of a theory, then it is
            %em provable
            from that theory. This is noticeably less trivial. In classical first-order logic, where the only models considered are set-valued ones, the completeness theorem is usually proven using
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraproduct"} ultraproduct
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo =
                  %mi Syn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T = Syn(T)
            of a theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            was mentioned above, as the left adjoint to the “internal logic functor”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lang
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            contains a “generic” model of the theory. Moreover, by the construction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %em precisely
            those provable from the theory. Therefore, if a sequent is valid in all models, it is in particular valid in the generic model in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            The universal property of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            is also sometimes useful for semantic conclusions. For instance, sometimes one can prove something about the generic model and then carry it over to all models.
          %p
            Furthermore, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            lives in a sub-fragment of geometric logic (such as regular, coherent, lextensive, or geometric logic), then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            for its appropriate (regular, coherent, extensive, or geometric)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            contains a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of the theory.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            of a theory can be considered as the “extensional essence” of that theory, since functors out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            completely determine the
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            with suitable structure. It therefore makes sense, in some contexts, to define a
            %em morphism
            of theories to be a functor between their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+categories"} syntactic categories
            %em equivalence
            of theories (sometimes called a
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Morita equivalence
          %p
            A morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to T'
            between theories, in this sense, induces a functor from
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            with suitable structure, in a way which is natural in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            with suitable structure; so they have the same “meaning” even though they may be presented quite differently. (Note that this is a much stronger sort of equivalence than merely having equivalent categories of models in some particular category, such as
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to T'
            actually induces a “translation” of the types, functions, and relations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            into those of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
          %p
            By first applying various “completion” processes to syntactic categories before asking about equivalence, we obtain coarser notions of equivalence, which only induce equivalences of models in more restricted sorts of categories. For instance, if we compare the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+completion"} exact completion
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h2#kripkejoyal_semantics Kripke–Joyal semantics
          %p
            To be written, but see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke-Joyal+semantics"} Kripke-Joyal semantics
          %h2#dependent_type_theory Dependent type theory
          %p
            We now consider the internal language of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          .standout
            %p
              Material to be moved here from
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h2#higherorder_logic Higher-order logic
          %p
            To be written, but see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell–Bénabou language
            for the version in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#LogicOfSet
            Internal logic in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            of course has as its internal logic the “ordinary” logic. This is reproduced by following the abstract nonsense as follows:
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            one-element set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is the two-element set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi true
                  %mo ,
                  %mi false
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega = \{true, false\}
            equipped with the map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
